from qlib import Q
C='brokerage-practice'
Q(C,'basic',"Which listing gives the broker a commission no matter who sells the property, even the owner?","Exclusive right-to-sell listing",[("Exclusive agency listing","Under exclusive agency, the owner owes no commission if the owner sells it alone."),("Open listing","An open listing pays only the broker who brings the buyer."),("Net listing","A net listing describes how the commission is figured, not who earns it.")],"Exclusive right-to-sell = commission whoever sells","In an **exclusive right-to-sell** listing, the broker earns the commission if the property sells during the term, **even if the owner finds the buyer**. It is the most common listing type.",5,["listing types"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Under an exclusive agency listing, the seller finds a buyer without the broker's help. The broker:","Earns no commission",[("Earns the full commission","That would be true under an exclusive right-to-sell listing."),("Earns half the commission","There is no automatic half commission."),("Can sue to stop the sale","The seller kept the right to sell alone.")],"Exclusive agency: owner may sell alone with no commission","With an **exclusive agency** listing, one broker is the only agent, but the **owner keeps the right to sell** the property alone without paying a commission.",4,["listing types"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Why is a net listing illegal or discouraged in many states?","It creates a conflict of interest because the broker keeps everything above the seller's net",[("It never sets a listing price","A net listing sets a net amount; the conflict is the problem."),("It cannot be put in writing","Net listings can be written; that is not the problem."),("It always pays the broker less","It can pay the broker much more than a normal commission.")],"Net listing = broker keeps the excess → conflict of interest","In a **net listing**, the seller names the amount they want to receive and the broker keeps anything above it. This tempts the broker to set a low net price, a **conflict of interest**, so many states prohibit or limit it.",4,["listing types"])
Q(C,'basic',"A licensee deposits a buyer's earnest money check in the licensee's personal checking account. This is called:","Commingling",[("Conversion","Conversion is using the client's money for personal purposes; this question describes mixing."),("Steering","Steering is a fair housing violation."),("Puffing","Puffing is exaggerated sales talk.")],"Mixing client funds with own funds = commingling","**Commingling** is mixing money that belongs to others with the licensee's own money. Earnest money must go into the broker's **escrow (trust) account** as state law requires. Spending it is **conversion**, which is even more serious.",5,["trust funds"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A buyer asks a salesperson whether a deed restriction allows a backyard business. The salesperson should:","Recommend that the buyer consult a real estate attorney",[("Explain the legal meaning of the restriction","Interpreting legal documents can be the unauthorized practice of law."),("Say it is probably fine so the sale can close","Guessing could be misrepresentation."),("Rewrite the deed restriction for the buyer","Licensees cannot draft legal documents like deeds.")],"Legal questions → refer to an attorney","Licensees must not give legal advice or draft legal documents; that is the **unauthorized practice of law**. Licensees should stay within their expertise and recommend an **attorney**, inspector, or other expert when needed.",4,["scope of practice"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Two competing brokerages in a town agree to charge the same commission rate. This is a violation of:","Antitrust laws (price fixing)",[("Fair housing laws","Fair housing laws concern discrimination."),("RESPA","RESPA concerns settlement services and kickbacks."),("The Statute of Frauds","The Statute of Frauds requires certain contracts to be in writing.")],"Competing brokers agreeing on rates = price fixing","Under federal **antitrust law (Sherman Act)**, competing brokers may not agree on commission rates (**price fixing**), divide markets (**market allocation**), or agree to boycott another broker (**group boycott**). Commissions are always negotiable between broker and client.",5,["antitrust"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Before calling a phone number to look for new listings, a salesperson should check:","The National Do Not Call Registry and the firm's internal do-not-call list",[("The local tax rolls","Tax rolls do not show whether a number may be called."),("The MLS sold data","MLS data does not show calling permission."),("Only the state's sex offender registry","That registry has nothing to do with calling rules.")],"Cold calls → check Do Not Call lists","Federal **Do Not Call** rules limit telemarketing calls. Before cold calling, licensees must check the national registry and the company's own list, and honor requests not to be called.",3,["Do Not Call"])
Q(C,'basic',"Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance protects a licensee against:","Claims of negligent mistakes in professional services",[("Fire damage to a client's home","That is covered by the owner's hazard insurance."),("Losses from intentional fraud","E&O insurance generally does not cover intentional wrongdoing."),("Defects in a buyer's title","Title insurance covers title defects.")],"E&O = coverage for professional mistakes","**Errors and omissions insurance** covers claims that a licensee made a careless mistake or left something out while providing services. It usually does not cover intentional fraud.",3,["risk management"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A broker can be held responsible for a salesperson's wrongful acts done within the scope of the salesperson's work because of:","Vicarious liability",[("Caveat emptor","Caveat emptor means \"let the buyer beware.\""),("Commingling","Commingling is mixing funds."),("Novation","Novation replaces a contract or party.")],"Broker answers for agents' acts = vicarious liability","Under **vicarious liability**, a broker may be liable for the acts of salespersons and employees done within the scope of their work. That is why brokers must **supervise** their licensees.",3,["risk management"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A salesperson is treated as an independent contractor. Which of the following is generally TRUE?","The broker does not withhold income or Social Security taxes from the salesperson's pay",[("The broker sets the salesperson's exact daily work hours","Controlling hours suggests an employee relationship."),("The salesperson is paid an hourly wage","Independent contractors are usually paid by commission, not by the hour."),("The broker must provide employee health insurance","Independent contractors are not given employee benefits.")],"Independent contractor: commission pay, no tax withholding","For IRS purposes, a real estate salesperson can be a **statutory nonemployee** (independent contractor) when the salesperson is licensed, substantially all pay is based on **sales** (commission) rather than hours worked, and a **written contract** says they will not be treated as an employee for federal tax purposes. The broker does not withhold taxes.",3,["independent contractor"])
C='fair-housing'
Q(C,'basic',"Which of the following is a protected class under the federal Fair Housing Act?","Familial status",[("Occupation","Occupation is not a federal protected class."),("Income level","Income level is not a federal protected class."),("Marital status","Marital status is protected under some state laws but not the federal Fair Housing Act.")],"Fair Housing Act: 7 federal protected classes","The federal **Fair Housing Act** protects seven classes: **race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability (handicap), and familial status** (households with children under 18 and pregnant women). Sexual orientation and gender identity are not listed in the federal Act, but many states and cities protect them under their own laws.",5,["protected classes"])
Q(C,'basic',"A salesperson shows buyers homes only in neighborhoods where most residents share the buyers' race. This illegal practice is:","Steering",[("Blockbusting","Blockbusting is urging owners to sell by saying people of a protected class are moving in."),("Redlining","Redlining is refusing loans or insurance in certain areas."),("Puffing","Puffing is exaggerated sales talk.")],"Guiding buyers by protected class = steering","**Steering** is directing buyers toward or away from areas based on a protected class. It is illegal even if the buyer does not complain.",5,["prohibited practices"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A lender refuses to make loans in a certain neighborhood because of the racial makeup of the area. This is:","Redlining",[("Steering","Steering is done by licensees guiding buyers."),("Blockbusting","Blockbusting is scaring owners into selling."),("Disintermediation","Disintermediation is money leaving banks for other investments.")],"Refusing loans by area = redlining","**Redlining** is refusing to make loans or provide insurance in a certain area because of the race or other protected class of the people who live there.",4,["prohibited practices"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A salesperson sends flyers saying, \"Sell now before values drop because new families of a certain religion are moving in.\" This is:","Blockbusting (panic peddling)",[("Steering","Steering is guiding buyers to or away from areas."),("Redlining","Redlining is done by lenders and insurers."),("Legal marketing","This is illegal under the Fair Housing Act.")],"Panic selling by protected class = blockbusting","**Blockbusting** (panic peddling) is urging owners to sell by saying that people of a protected class are moving into the neighborhood and values will fall.",4,["prohibited practices"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A tenant who uses a wheelchair asks to install grab bars in the bathroom at the tenant's own expense. Under the Fair Housing Act, the landlord:","Must allow the reasonable modification, and may require restoration when the tenant moves out in some cases",[("May refuse because the tenant is changing the unit","The law requires allowing reasonable modifications."),("Must pay for the grab bars","The tenant generally pays for modifications in private housing."),("May charge a higher security deposit for disability","Extra deposits because of disability are not allowed.")],"Disability: reasonable modifications (tenant pays) and accommodations","The Fair Housing Act requires landlords to allow **reasonable modifications** to the unit, usually at the tenant's expense, and to make **reasonable accommodations** in rules, such as allowing an assistance animal in a no-pets building. A landlord may in some cases require the interior to be restored at move-out.",4,["disability"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which advertisement would most likely violate fair housing laws?","\"Perfect for a mature couple, no kids\"",[("\"Three bedrooms, two baths, near schools\"","Describing the property is allowed."),("\"Walking distance to the train station\"","Describing location features is allowed."),("\"Wheelchair-accessible entrance\"","Describing accessibility features is allowed.")],"Ads must describe the property, not the buyers","Fair housing ads must describe the **property**, not the kind of people wanted. \"No kids\" shows a preference based on **familial status**, which is illegal unless the property qualifies as housing for older persons.",4,["advertising"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A person who believes they suffered housing discrimination may file a complaint with HUD within:","One year of the alleged violation",[("30 days of the alleged violation","The deadline is longer than 30 days."),("Five years of the alleged violation","Five years is too long for a HUD complaint."),("Two years only after first suing in court","A HUD complaint does not require a lawsuit first.")],"HUD complaint: within 1 year; federal lawsuit: within 2 years","Under the federal Fair Housing Act, a complaint may be filed with **HUD** within **one year** of the alleged discrimination. A private lawsuit in federal court may be filed within **two years**.",3,["enforcement"])
Q(C,'advanced',"The Civil Rights Act of 1866 is important in real estate because it prohibits:","All racial discrimination in property transactions, with no exceptions",[("Discrimination based on familial status","Familial status was added by the 1988 amendments to the Fair Housing Act."),("Discrimination based on disability only","Disability protection came from later laws."),("Redlining by lenders only","The 1866 Act covers all property transactions, not only lending.")],"1866 Act = race, no exceptions","The **Civil Rights Act of 1866** bars **all discrimination based on race** in buying, selling, or renting property, with no exceptions. The Fair Housing Act's exemptions, such as for some owner-occupied buildings, do not apply to race under the 1866 Act.",3,["fair housing laws"])
C='disclosures-environmental'
Q(C,'basic',"For homes built before what year must sellers and landlords disclose known lead-based paint hazards?","1978",[("1950","The federal rule covers homes built before 1978, not 1950."),("1990","1990 is too late; lead paint for homes was banned in 1978."),("1965","The cutoff year is 1978.")],"Pre-1978 housing → lead-based paint disclosure","Federal law requires sellers and landlords of **housing built before 1978** to disclose known lead-based paint and hazards, give the EPA pamphlet, and keep a lead warning statement in the contract. Buyers get a **10-day** chance to test, unless they waive it.",5,["lead-based paint"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Under the federal lead-based paint rule, a buyer of a pre-1978 home is usually given how long to conduct a lead inspection or risk assessment?","10 days, unless the buyer waives it or the parties agree to another period",[("3 days","The standard period is 10 days."),("30 days","The standard period is 10 days."),("No inspection period is provided","Buyers are given an opportunity to test.")],"Lead paint: 10-day inspection opportunity","Buyers of pre-1978 housing have a **10-day** opportunity to inspect for lead-based paint hazards. The parties may agree in writing to a different period, or the buyer may waive it.",4,["lead-based paint"])
Q(C,'basic',"Radon is best described as:","A colorless, odorless radioactive gas that can enter homes from the soil",[("A fiber once used in insulation and floor tiles","That describes asbestos."),("A toxic metal found in old paint","That describes lead."),("A fungus that grows in damp areas","That describes mold.")],"Radon = odorless radioactive gas from soil","**Radon** is a radioactive gas from the natural breakdown of uranium in soil and rock. It has no color or smell and can only be found by testing. Long exposure is linked to lung cancer. It can be reduced with a mitigation system.",4,["radon"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Asbestos is most dangerous when it is:","Damaged or disturbed so that fibers get into the air",[("Left undisturbed and in good condition","Undisturbed asbestos in good condition is generally less risky."),("Painted over","Painting (encapsulation) can help keep fibers in place."),("Covered by flooring","Covering it can reduce release of fibers.")],"Asbestos hazard = airborne fibers when disturbed","**Asbestos** was used in insulation, siding, and flooring. It is mainly dangerous when it becomes **friable** (crumbly) or is disturbed, letting fibers into the air. Removal can release more fibers, so **encapsulation** is sometimes preferred.",4,["asbestos"])
Q(C,'advanced',"The federal law that can hold current owners liable for cleaning up hazardous waste even if they did not cause it is:","CERCLA (Superfund)",[("The Clean Air Act","The Clean Air Act regulates air pollution."),("RESPA","RESPA regulates settlement procedures."),("The Fair Housing Act","The Fair Housing Act concerns discrimination.")],"CERCLA = strict, joint and several cleanup liability","**CERCLA**, known as **Superfund**, makes present and past owners and others liable for cleanup of hazardous waste sites. Liability can be **strict** (no fault needed), **joint and several**, and **retroactive**. SARA later amended it and added the innocent landowner defense.",4,["CERCLA"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A buyer of commercial land wants to qualify for the innocent landowner defense under federal environmental law. The buyer should first obtain a:","Phase I environmental site assessment",[("Comparative market analysis","A CMA estimates price, not contamination."),("Certificate of occupancy","A certificate of occupancy concerns building use."),("Termite inspection","A termite inspection checks for wood-destroying insects.")],"Phase I ESA = due diligence for contamination","A **Phase I environmental site assessment** reviews records and the site for signs of contamination. Doing proper due diligence before buying helps a buyer claim the **innocent landowner** defense. If problems are found, a **Phase II** with sampling may follow.",3,["environmental site assessment"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A home is in a special flood hazard area and the buyer is getting a federally backed mortgage. What is generally required?","Flood insurance",[("A radon test","Radon testing is not required because of a flood zone."),("A Phase II environmental assessment","That is not required for a home in a flood zone."),("A lead-based paint abatement","Lead abatement is unrelated to flood zones.")],"Federally backed loan in flood zone → flood insurance","When a property is in a **special flood hazard area** on FEMA maps and the loan is from a federally regulated or insured lender, **flood insurance** is generally required.",4,["flood"])
Q(C,'basic',"A material fact is best described as a fact that:","Would affect a reasonable buyer's decision to buy or the price paid",[("Only the seller thinks is important","Material facts are judged by what a reasonable person would think."),("Appears only in the public records","Material facts may or may not be in public records."),("Has been fixed and no longer matters","A repaired problem may still need disclosure under state law.")],"Material fact = would change a reasonable buyer's decision","A **material fact** is information that would affect a reasonable person's decision to buy, or the price or terms. Licensees must disclose known material facts about a property's condition.",5,["material facts"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which of the following is NOT generally treated as a material physical defect that must be disclosed?","The seller's reason for moving to another state",[("A cracked foundation","A cracked foundation is a physical defect that must be disclosed."),("A roof that leaks","A leaking roof must be disclosed."),("A failing septic system","A failing septic system must be disclosed.")],"Physical defects must be disclosed; personal motives are confidential","Known **physical defects** such as foundation cracks, roof leaks, and septic failure must be disclosed. The seller's **personal reason for moving** is not a physical defect and is usually **confidential** information the seller's agent should not reveal.",4,["material facts"],lock=True)
Q(C,'intermediate',"A salesperson says, \"This is the best view in the whole city!\" This statement is most likely:","Puffing",[("Fraud","Fraud is a false statement of a material fact made to deceive."),("Negligent misrepresentation","Misrepresentation involves a false statement of fact, not opinion."),("Steering","Steering is a fair housing violation.")],"Opinion/exaggeration = puffing; false fact = misrepresentation","**Puffing** is exaggerated opinion that a reasonable person would not take as fact. It is different from **misrepresentation**, a false statement of fact, and **fraud**, an intentional false statement made to deceive. Licensees should still be careful with puffing.",3,["misrepresentation"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Underground storage tanks (USTs) are a concern mainly because they can:","Leak petroleum or chemicals into soil and groundwater",[("Produce radon gas","Radon comes from natural uranium in soil and rock."),("Release asbestos fibers","Asbestos is a building material."),("Cause lead-based paint hazards","Lead is in old paint, not storage tanks.")],"USTs → leaks into soil and groundwater","**Underground storage tanks** for fuel or chemicals can rust and **leak** into soil and **groundwater**. Old tanks may need to be tested or removed, and cleanup can be costly.",3,["underground storage tanks"])
C='financing-settlement'
Q(C,'basic',"In a mortgage loan, the borrower is the:","Mortgagor",[("Mortgagee","The mortgagee is the lender."),("Trustee","A trustee holds title under a deed of trust."),("Beneficiary","The beneficiary is the lender under a deed of trust.")],"Mortgagor = borrower; mortgagee = lender","The borrower gives the mortgage, so the borrower is the **mortgagor**. The lender receives it and is the **mortgagee**. With a **deed of trust**, the borrower is the trustor, the lender is the beneficiary, and a third party is the trustee.",5,["mortgage terms"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which loan clause lets the lender demand the full loan balance if the borrower sells the property?","Alienation (due-on-sale) clause",[("Acceleration clause","An acceleration clause makes the debt due on default, such as missed payments."),("Prepayment clause","A prepayment clause concerns paying the loan off early."),("Defeasance clause","A defeasance clause requires the lender to release the lien when the loan is paid.")],"Sale → due-on-sale (alienation) clause","An **alienation (due-on-sale) clause** lets the lender call the whole loan due when the property is sold or transferred, which prevents a buyer from assuming the loan without permission. An **acceleration clause** makes the debt due when the borrower defaults.",4,["mortgage clauses"])
Q(C,'basic',"Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac are part of the:","Secondary mortgage market",[("Primary mortgage market","The primary market is where lenders make loans to borrowers."),("Federal Reserve System","They are not part of the Federal Reserve."),("FHA insurance program","FHA is a government agency that insures loans.")],"Secondary market buys loans from lenders","In the **primary market**, lenders make loans to borrowers. In the **secondary market**, investors such as **Fannie Mae** and **Freddie Mac** buy those loans, giving lenders money to make more loans. **Ginnie Mae** guarantees securities backed by FHA and VA loans.",4,["secondary market"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which type of loan is guaranteed (partly) by the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs and may allow no down payment for eligible borrowers?","VA loan",[("FHA loan","FHA loans are insured by the Federal Housing Administration and require a down payment."),("Conventional loan","Conventional loans are not government guaranteed."),("Reverse mortgage","A reverse mortgage lets older owners borrow against equity.")],"VA = guaranteed, eligible veterans, often no down payment","**VA loans** are partly **guaranteed** by the Department of Veterans Affairs for eligible veterans and service members, and may require **no down payment**. **FHA loans** are **insured** and need a low down payment plus mortgage insurance premium (MIP).",4,["government loans"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"On a conventional loan, private mortgage insurance (PMI) is usually required when the loan-to-value ratio is:","More than 80%",[("Less than 50%","Low LTV loans do not normally need PMI."),("Exactly 60%","60% LTV does not normally need PMI."),("Less than 80%","PMI is required when the LTV is above 80%, not below.")],"Conventional loan > 80% LTV → PMI","**Private mortgage insurance (PMI)** protects the lender. It is usually required on a conventional loan when the borrower puts down less than 20%, so the **LTV is above 80%**.",5,["PMI"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Under the TILA-RESPA Integrated Disclosure (TRID) rule, when must the borrower receive the Closing Disclosure?","At least 3 business days before consummation (closing)",[("At least 10 days before applying for the loan","The Closing Disclosure is given near closing, not before applying."),("Within 3 business days after closing","It must be given before closing."),("On the day of closing only","Same-day delivery is too late.")],"Closing Disclosure: 3 business days before closing","Under **TRID**, the lender gives the **Loan Estimate** within **3 business days after application**, and the borrower must receive the **Closing Disclosure** at least **3 business days before closing**.",5,["TRID"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A title company pays a salesperson $100 for each buyer the salesperson refers to it, without any services in return. This violates:","RESPA's ban on kickbacks for referrals",[("The Equal Credit Opportunity Act","ECOA concerns discrimination in lending."),("The Fair Housing Act","Fair housing concerns discrimination in housing."),("The Truth in Lending Act advertising rules","TILA advertising rules cover credit terms in ads.")],"Referral fee for settlement services = RESPA violation","**RESPA** prohibits kickbacks, fees, or anything of value given for referring settlement services (title, lending, escrow) on federally related mortgage loans. Payment must be for services actually performed.",4,["RESPA"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A home loan ad says, \"Only 5% down!\" Under Truth in Lending (Regulation Z), what else must the ad state?","The terms of repayment and the annual percentage rate (APR)",[("Nothing else, because a percentage is not a trigger term","A specific down payment amount or percentage is a trigger term."),("The seller's name and address","The seller's identity is not a Regulation Z requirement."),("The broker's commission rate","Commission is not a credit term.")],"Trigger term in an ad → disclose down payment, repayment terms, APR","Under **Regulation Z**, specific credit terms in an ad are **trigger terms**, such as the amount or percentage of the down payment, the number of payments, the payment amount, or the finance charge. Using one requires the ad to also state the down payment, the **terms of repayment**, and the **APR**. General phrases like \"easy terms\" do not trigger these disclosures.",4,["Regulation Z"])
Q(C,'basic',"The Equal Credit Opportunity Act (ECOA) prohibits lenders from discriminating based on:","Marital status or age (if the applicant can legally contract)",[("The borrower's credit history","Lenders may consider credit history."),("The borrower's income","Lenders may consider income."),("The property's appraised value","Lenders may consider value.")],"ECOA adds marital status, age, public assistance","**ECOA** bars credit discrimination based on race, color, religion, national origin, sex, **marital status**, **age** (if old enough to contract), and receipt of public assistance. Lenders may still judge creditworthiness, such as income and credit history.",4,["ECOA"])
Q(C,'advanced',"In a land contract (contract for deed), the buyer usually:","Takes possession and pays the seller in installments, but legal title stays with the seller until paid in full or as agreed",[("Receives legal title at closing","Legal title stays with the seller under a land contract."),("Must get a bank loan before moving in","The seller finances the sale."),("Has no interest in the property until the last payment","The buyer has equitable title and possession.")],"Land contract: seller keeps legal title; buyer has equitable title","With a **land contract (contract for deed, installment sales contract)**, the **seller** provides financing and keeps **legal title**. The buyer (vendee) has possession and **equitable title** and receives the deed after paying as agreed.",4,["seller financing"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"In a lien theory state, a mortgage:","Creates a lien on the property while the borrower keeps title",[("Gives legal title to the lender until paid","That describes title theory."),("Transfers title to a trustee","That describes a deed of trust."),("Cannot be foreclosed","Mortgages in lien theory states can be foreclosed, usually through the courts.")],"Lien theory: borrower holds title; title theory: lender holds title","In a **lien theory** state, the borrower keeps title and the mortgage is a **lien**. In a **title theory** state, the lender (or a trustee) holds title until the loan is paid.",3,["lien theory"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which document shows a borrower's promise to repay a loan?","Promissory note",[("Deed","A deed transfers ownership of real property."),("Mortgage","The mortgage pledges the property as security."),("Closing Disclosure","The Closing Disclosure shows final loan costs.")],"Note = promise to pay; mortgage = security","The **promissory note** is the borrower's promise to repay the debt. The **mortgage** (or deed of trust) pledges the property as **security** for the note.",4,["notes"])
